@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Single Galaxy: Tiered Colony Simulation / Distant Event Batching
+Date: 2026-11-01; gate ECON-03c2
+
+## Player-facing rule
+Distant colonies do not pause and do not run physically detailed NPC loops. Their
+consumption, output and movements remain ledgered against world time. The detail
+budget is *not* a change to the passage of game time.
+
+## Proposed level-of-detail bands (heuristic, not a final tuning)
+- **Focused:** player's immediate site/ship. Full relevant local agent, spatial,
+  physics, construction, hazards and tools. Preserve authoritative mutations.
+- **Nearby:** visible systems and imminent player contacts. Limited full agents;
+  abstract repeated routine tasks; synchronize authoritative cross-boundary actions.
+- **Remote:** non-visible colonies. Aggregate by resource compartment, population
+  cohort, jobs, supply routes, utilities and event deadlines. No object-by-object
+  render/transport loop. Event-driven output using shared deterministic kernels.
+- **Dormant:** far away, stable districts with no active interaction. Apply exact
+  bulk transformations between hard event boundaries, instead of calling
+  Simulation::step() for every missed tick. Only activate fine detail when needed.
+
+Suggested scheduler wake-up reasons: earliest food/water/oxygen deadline where a
+shortfall or policy consequence can occur, ship arrival, cargo receipt, scheduled
+production finish, power/fuel exhaustion, construction completion, migration,
+attack, player command, or forced reconciliation. Quiet stretches can be very long;
+the world clock remains common.
+
+## Exactness boundary
+- Any component eligible for batching must have a validated aggregate formula
+  identical to the detailed update over the skipped interval.
+- At a change in input, population, fuel, power or threat, split the time interval
+  and evaluate the event in strict, persistent world order.
+- Never simulate deliveries as simply positive inventory: all real material
+  transfers must debit an authorized source and credit one destination exactly once.
+- Missed supplies are shortages, not invisible debt; refuse impossible outputs.
+- For named NPCs, maintain durable life histories and persistent records. Macro
+  cohorts are the economical default, not a reason to destroy existing identities.
+- Enforce checked integer bounds, resumable checkpoints, and split vs continuous
+  update equivalence. If any component is not proven safe for batching, keep it
+  detailed until its aggregate transition is implemented and tested.
+- Approaching player views must hydrate detailed agents and equipment from the
+  authoritative macro state; never regenerate arbitrary wealth or duplicate NPCs.
+
+## ECON-03c2 implemented scope
+`ColdColonyNeeds` models one remote population, a local habitat life-support
+compartment, and a physically distinct depot. It algebraically advances any number
+of due ration windows in O(event count + 1), with same-tick arrival/population
+changes before the needs deadline, bounded source-to-consumer deliveries,
+shortage counts and a copy/commit transaction boundary for failed inputs.
+
+This kernel is intentionally NOT connected to the normal `Simulation::step()`
+because that live world includes mining, factories, utility power, and other
+side effects that cannot yet be safely skipped. Whole-colony event queue,
+production/utility/transport integration, durable SQL event checkpoints,
+workload benchmarks and wake-up promotion/demotion remain follow-up gates.
